### What happened?

LiteLLM bug: Headroom on `/v1/responses` rewrites Codex MCP tools into Claude-style names, so Codex cannot run them.

### What works:

Codex talks to LiteLLM with wire_api = "responses". MCP servers are registered locally. Codex sends those as MCP tools. Without Headroom, Codex can fetch JIRA (<server_name>-<tool_name> locally).

### What breaks

Send the same Codex request with X-Headroom-Compression: true. LiteLLM runs the headroom-compression guardrail. After that guardrail, the tool list is no longer MCP servers. It is a long list of Claude/Anthropic-style function names, for example:

`mcp__<server_name>__<server_name>_<tool_name>`

The model then calls the tool with the unknown name format. Codex does not have a tool by that name. Codex returns:

unsupported call: `mcp__<server_name>__<server_name>_<tool_name>`

MCP tool never runs. Headroom never sees a large tool result. Compression/retrieve never happens.

### What we measured

On one Headroom-on Codex /v1/responses request (call_type=aresponses, model gpt-5.3-codex):

`
Stage | Tool names
-- | --
Into LiteLLM (Codex body) | mcp__<server_name> plus Codex builtins
After Headroom apply_guardrail | `mcp__<server>__<server>_<tool>`, … (every MCP tool flattened)
`

A plain `/v1/responses` call that already had a hyphenated function name `<server_name>-<tool_name> ` (no Codex MCP) did not get renamed. The bug is specifically: Headroom expands Responses MCP tools.

### What we expect

Headroom may compress messages and inject headroom_retrieve for LiteLLM to run server-side. It must not change Codex MCP tool names into `mcp__<server>__<server>_<tool>`. The client must still see the same MCP/function names it registered.

### User Flow

### Before a (hypothetical) fix

The flow breaks at the first tool call after the `headroom-compression` guardrail is enabled: the agent's tool call comes back rejected as an unsupported name, so no data is ever fetched.

In the CLI's config file, I point the model provider at my gateway, `https://llm-proxy.example.com,` set my key to `user=<sso>&key=<key>`, and register one remote MCP server at https://llm-proxy.example.com/mcp/tools_server with my auth headers.

I let the CLI list what it can reach. It issues POST `https://llm-proxy.example.com/mcp/tools_server` (a tools/list request) and gets `200` back with the server's tools, shown to me as `tools_server-get_record`.

I enable the `headroom-compression` guardrail on that same provider so every chat request through this CLI runs with Headroom on.

I start the CLI and ask it, in plain language, to fetch record `REC-1001 `and summarize it.

The CLI sends POST `https://llm-proxy.example.com/v1/responses` with my prompt, its own built-in tools, and its MCP server entry (`mcp__tools_server`). I get 200 and a normal streamed turn.

The agent's turn shows a tool attempt and an immediate rejection: it reports calling `tools_server-get_record` but the result text is unsupported call: `mcp__tools_server__tools_server_get_record` — a name I never registered, with the server name doubled inside it. `REC-1001` is never retrieved.

The agent gives up and answers from nothing: "record contents unavailable." I never see record data, only the rejection line.

I check Headroom's own counters at GET `http://127.0.0.1:8787/stats`. Requests received is non-zero (4 on my run), but compressed requests and retrievals are both 0 — nothing large ever arrived to compress.

I turn the `headroom-compression` guardrail off and rerun the identical prompt. The CLI calls `tools_server-get_record`, gets the record body, and summarizes it correctly.

### After a (hypothetical) fix

The same flow completes with the `headroom-compression `guardrail on: the tool call runs under the name the CLI registered, and the fetched record is compressed and restored inside one request.

In the CLI's config file, I point the model provider at my gateway, `https://llm-proxy.example.com`, set my key to `user=<sso>&key=<key>`, and register one remote MCP server at `https://llm-proxy.example.com/mcp/tools_server` with my auth headers.

I let the CLI list what it can reach. It issues POST` https://llm-proxy.example.com/mcp/tools_server `(a tools/list request) and gets 200 back with the server's tools, shown to me as `tools_server-get_record`.
I enable the headroom-compression guardrail on that same provider so every chat request through this CLI runs with Headroom on.

I start the CLI and ask it, in plain language, to fetch record `REC-1001` and summarize it.

The CLI sends POST `https://llm-proxy.example.com/v1/responses` with my prompt, its own built-in tools, and its MCP server entry (`mcp__tools_server`). I get 200 and a normal streamed turn.

The agent's turn shows the tool actually running under the name I registered, `tools_server-get_record`, and the record body comes back. No unsupported call line appears.

The agent summarizes `REC-1001` from the retrieved content, quoting detail that only exists in the record body.
I check Headroom's own counters at GET `http://127.0.0.1:8787/stats`. Requests received is non-zero, and compressed requests and retrievals have both increased, so the large tool result was shrunk and the needed part restored during that same turn. I am never asked to run a retrieval tool myself.

I turn the `headroom-compression` guardrail off and rerun the identical prompt. It still works, just with no token reduction — matching the behavior from step 9 above.

The consequence here is availability, not authorization: no other user gains access to anything, and no data crosses accounts. Turning `headroom-compression` on silently disables every remote tool the CLI registered, so an agent that looks healthy (HTTP 200, a fluent answer) is actually answering without its data sources, and the token savings the guardrail was enabled for never materialize.

